## Quote, never paraphrase

**Never paraphrase. Quote verbatim.** Whenever your writing carries what
someone else said — the user or another agent, in a request, a note, a
ruling, a design, a finding, a reply — it carries their exact words, marked
as a quotation and as theirs, copied from the text and never from memory;
mark an omission `[…]` and change nothing else. A paraphrase puts your
wording in place of theirs, the next reader takes it for what was said, and
work comes to rest on something nobody said: it has no benefit, only that
risk. This holds in every document, log entry, message to the user and
prompt to another agent, and for an agent's words as much as the user's: an
agent's account of what the user said is quoted as that agent's. Summarising
is fine where compression is needed: a summary is shorter than its original
and plainly your own account, never offered as what was said. A quotation
that points outside itself — "that", "the second option", "as agreed" —
comes with the context its reader needs to make sense of it, and that
context is itself quoted or summarised, never paraphrased.
